💭 A 🔺 around a call that raises a subclass of the error which its own function raises.
💭
💭 Expected: it compiles without a word, and the program prints "of the subclass".
💭 What happens: the compiler prints "Call parameter type does not match function
💭 signature!" and "Detected in:" with the function's name, which is LLVM's verifier
💭 refusing the code, and then ends with status 0 as if all were well. 🔺 hands its own
💭 function's error pointer to the call as it is (ASTReraise::generate), and the call
💭 was declared with a pointer to the subclass. The program that was built runs here,
💭 and prints the line. A build script cannot tell that the verifier refused anything
💭 without reading what the compiler printed.
